---
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';

type Value = [number, number] | [number];
type Display = { years: number[]; states: string[]; groups: string[]; values: Value[][][] };
const root = 'data/participation-cps-state-history/20261107-01/output';
const display: Display = JSON.parse(readFileSync(`${root}/display.json`, 'utf8'));
const archive: { bytes: number; sha256: string } = JSON.parse(readFileSync(`${root}/archive.json`, 'utf8'));
const release = 'https://github.com/hanshanley/margin_of_error/releases/download/participation-cps-state-history-20261107-01';
const statuses: Record<number, string> = {
	'-1': 'Not published (B)',
	'-2': 'Withheld: small population base',
	'-3': 'Zero or rounds to zero',
};
const text = (value: Value) => value.length === 2
	? `${value[0].toFixed(1)}% +/- ${value[1].toFixed(1)} pp` : statuses[value[0]];
const groups = [
	['Overall', [0]],
	['Source-reported sex', [1, 2]],
	['Race and Hispanic origin: overlapping views', [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10]],
	['Age at each election', [11, 12, 13, 14, 15]],
] as const;
---

<section id="participation-cps-state-history" class="participation-current" aria-labelledby="cps-state-title">
	<header class="section-intro">
		<p class="kicker">Participation by state and group · Census published estimates</p>
		<h2 id="cps-state-title">Who reported voting in midterms and presidential elections?</h2>
		<p>Compare <strong>all 50 states and D.C.</strong> in 2018, 2020, 2022 and 2024.
			These are native Census estimates of <strong>reported voting among adult citizens</strong>,
			not candidate preference, administrative turnout or 2026 outcomes.</p>
	</header>
	<p>Each cell shows the published percentage and its <strong>90% margin of error in percentage points</strong>.
		For example, 58.7% +/- 4.5 pp means the source estimate plus or minus 4.5 points.
		These margins do not cover nonsampling error or establish whether a difference is statistically significant.</p>
	<div data-cps-controls hidden>
		<label for="cps-state-group">Demographic view
			<select id="cps-state-group">
				{groups.map(([label, indices]) => <optgroup label={label}>
					{indices.map(index => <option value={index}>{index === 0 ? 'All adult citizens' : display.groups[index]}</option>)}
				</optgroup>)}
			</select>
		</label>
	</div>
	<p class="caption" id="cps-state-selection" aria-live="polite">All adult citizens. Age and group membership are measured anew each election, not fixed-person cohorts.</p>
	<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="State reported participation, with native 90% margins">
		<table>
			<caption>Reported voting among adult citizens: percentage +/- native 90% margin (pp).
				The national benchmark includes D.C.; do not add it to the state rows.</caption>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Geography</th>
				{display.years.map(year => <th scope="col">{year}<br />{year % 4 === 0 ? 'Presidential' : 'Midterm'}</th>)}
			</tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				{display.states.map((state, index) => <tr data-cps-state={index}>
					<th scope="row">{state}</th>
					{display.values[index][0].map((value, year) => <td data-cps-year={year}>{text(value)}</td>)}
				</tr>)}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p><strong>Missing is not zero.</strong> Native “B” means the source did not publish the rate because
		its population base was too small. We also withhold display rates when the rounded citizen-population
		base is 75,000 or less, including the ambiguous rounding boundary. This is <strong>population, not
		sample size</strong>, and passing it does not guarantee precision. Neither rates nor margins
		are reconstructed from rounded counts.</p>
	<details class="explorer-notes" data-cps-methods>
		<summary>Definitions, comparability and complete data</summary>
		<p>Sources are the U.S. Census Bureau’s November CPS Voting and Registration Supplement,
			published state tables 4b (sex, race and Hispanic origin) and 4c (age) for
			<a href="https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/cps/tables/p20/583/">2018</a>,
			<a href="https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/cps/tables/p20/585/">2020</a>,
			<a href="https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/cps/tables/p20/586/">2022</a> and
			<a href="https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/cps/tables/p20/587/">2024</a>.
			The denominator is the civilian noninstitutionalized adult citizen population,
			not a legal-eligibility count. Voting is self/proxy-reported and may be over-reported.</p>
		<p>The source’s “alone,” “alone or in combination,” and Hispanic-origin views overlap.
			Do not add them, infer an exclusive residual, or combine sex, age and race marginals
			into intersections. Hispanic “of any race” and “any race” are the same labeled view here;
			other native labels are retained in the complete transcription. Education, income and
			urbanicity are not in these state tables.</p>
		<p>Native 2018/2020 “B” suppression is preserved. The 2022/2024 workbooks publish some
			numeric rates for smaller population bases; the additional display rule applies consistently
			across all four years. Complete, unmodified governmental aggregate workbooks and native-cell
			transcriptions retain their original values and flags. No suppressed rate is recovered.
			A native dash means zero or rounds to zero, not an exact inferred zero.</p>
		<p>Counts in the CSV are weighted population estimates <strong>in thousands</strong>, not
			survey respondent counts. Counts need not sum because of rounding. The duplicate overall
			controls in both tables agree for all 52 geographies in each year. Native national values
			include D.C.; no 50-state-only aggregate is manufactured from rounded counts.</p>
		<p>Repeated cross-sections, changing population controls, survey response and election
			environments do not identify individual transitions or causal midterm effects.
			No 2026 switching, staying-home, first-time-voter, county or precinct estimate is
			derived from this table. The margins are not simultaneous intervals and are never
			pooled into unsupported confidence intervals for changes.</p>
		<p>The complete download includes all 3,328 unique observations, eight original public
			aggregate workbooks, four frozen catalogs, source timestamps and checksums, native notes,
			code, tests and results. Extract it and run
			<code>python3 scripts/package-cps-state-history.py</code> to reproduce offline without
			microdata, network access or added Python packages.</p>
	</details>
	<div class="download-links">
		<a href={`${release}/complete-analysis.zip`} data-cps-download="complete" download>Complete state/group history + original aggregate inputs + code (ZIP)</a>
		<a href={`${release}/observations.csv`} data-cps-download="observations" download>All state/group historical observations and native cells (CSV)</a>
	</div>
	<p class="caption">Complete ZIP: {archive.bytes.toLocaleString('en-US')} bytes.
		SHA-256: <code class="checksum">{archive.sha256}</code>.
		Without JavaScript, the all-citizen table and both complete downloads remain available.</p>
	<script is:inline type="application/json" id="cps-state-history-data" set:html={JSON.stringify(display).replace(/</g, '\\u003c')} />
</section>

<script>
	const section = document.getElementById('participation-cps-state-history');
	const payload = document.getElementById('cps-state-history-data');
	const select = document.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('#cps-state-group');
	if (section && payload && select) {
		type Display = { groups: string[]; values: number[][][][] };
		const data: Display = JSON.parse(payload.textContent || '{}');
		const statuses: Record<number, string> = {
			'-1': 'Not published (B)',
			'-2': 'Withheld: small population base',
			'-3': 'Zero or rounds to zero',
		};
		select.addEventListener('change', () => {
			const group = Number(select.value);
			section.querySelectorAll<HTMLTableRowElement>('[data-cps-state]').forEach(row => {
				const state = Number(row.dataset.cpsState);
				row.querySelectorAll<HTMLTableCellElement>('[data-cps-year]').forEach(cell => {
					const value = data.values[state][group][Number(cell.dataset.cpsYear)];
					cell.textContent = value.length === 2
						? `${value[0].toFixed(1)}% +/- ${value[1].toFixed(1)} pp`
						: statuses[value[0]];
				});
			});
			const note = document.getElementById('cps-state-selection');
			if (note) note.textContent = `${group === 0 ? 'All adult citizens' : data.groups[group]}. Age and group membership are measured anew each election, not fixed-person cohorts.`;
		});
		const controls = section.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-cps-controls]');
		if (controls) controls.hidden = false;
	}
</script>

<style>
	#participation-cps-state-history { min-width: 0; margin-block: 1.5rem 2rem; }
	.table-wrap { max-width: 100%; overflow-x: auto; }
	table { width: 100%; min-width: 46rem; }
	td { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
	select { max-width: 100%; }
	.checksum { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
